<h2>Why the design is more than a collection of games</h2>
<p>The product separates immediate gameplay feedback from long-lived educational progress. A game can present a prompt or challenge, but the curriculum layer needs to track which standard or skill was exercised, the quality of the evidence, and when review is appropriate.</p>
<p>The repository includes distinct responsibilities for Phaser scenes and systems; React quiz/learning interfaces; curriculum application, domain, and infrastructure modules; and student identity and persistence. The source models question templates, attempts, skill evidence, diagnostic placement, mastery state, and review schedules.</p>
<h2>Architecture and data flow</h2>
<pre class="mermaid">flowchart TD
  L[Learner browser] --> R[React lessons and quiz UI]
  L --> P[Phaser game scenes and systems]
  R --> A[NestJS API]
  P --> A
  A --> I[Student identity]
  A --> C[Curriculum application services]
  C --> D[Standards, questions, mastery policies]
  I --> DB[(PostgreSQL via Prisma)]
  C --> DB
</pre>
<p>This diagram is a conceptual grouping of source modules, not an assertion that every game scene makes each API call directly.</p>
<h2>Important engineering decisions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Game architecture:</strong> scene composition stays distinct from shared gameplay entities and systems, so new mechanics need not inherit Fossil Dig-specific implementation details.</li>
<li><strong>Curriculum as data:</strong> the system stores standards, question templates and attempt evidence separately from rendering, supporting multiple game experiences and evolving content.</li>
<li><strong>Student-aware progress:</strong> the API derives identity from signed learner authentication rather than trusting a caller-supplied learner ID.</li>
<li><strong>Mastery and review:</strong> evidence and review state are explicitly persisted, with adult-controlled mastery checks documented for the learning workflow.</li>
<li><strong>Verification:</strong> the repository includes Vitest, Jest and Playwright coverage, plus separate curriculum validation and simulation tooling.</li>
</ul>
